ed D6. It was the first move to cost real points in a game that had been close and quiet."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "The 20k's C4 had just left Black at +25.5, the biggest lead of the game. GPT-6.1 Sol's E6 lost 20.6 points and dropped it to +4.9, where the engine wanted B5. A huge gift was mostly handed back at once."}, {"turn": 21, "text": "F4 is the worst move of the game, losing 24.7 points and turning +9.7 into -15.0. The engine's choice was B5. Black never got ahead again."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "J7 stretched Black's row-7 wall out to the edge and lost 23.6 points, taking the lead from -12.1 to -35.6. Black's last four moves along row 7, F7, G7, H7 and J7, lost 13.3, 10.9, 15.7 and 23.6 points. Each one answered a White move along row 8 while the engine's first choice stayed B5."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "The 20k answered with J8, the biggest loss of its game at 21.3 points, taking the lead from -35.6 to -14.3. It was a rare chance to lean on a mistake, but even this left Black 14.3 points behind. The crawl ended with Black's row-7 wall under a full White row 8."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every script (the `inspect` parse/groups/audit functions, board and play calls, replay/SGF building, file reads and notes) only inspects the current board or the one move the player named, computing its liberties and captures, or keeps records. None loops over empty points to score moves or plays continuations forward, so the player chose its own moves.", "stop": false}}